<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <Favicons />
    <title>{metaTitle}</title>
    <meta name="description" content={metaDescription} />
    <link rel="canonical" href="/seo/backlinks/gadget-posting-sites" />
    <meta property="og:type" content="article" />
    <meta property="og:title" content={metaTitle} />
    <meta property="og:description" content={metaDescription} />
    <JsonLd graph={graph} />
  </head>
  <body>
    <SiteHeader />

    <main>
      <section
        class="page-header pillar-hero"
        style="--topic: rgba(241,94,74,0.16); --topic-fg: #d8412c;"
      >
        <div class="container">
          <div class="breadcrumbs">
            <a href="/">Home</a>
            <span>&#8250;</span>
            <a href="/seo">SEO</a>
            <span>&#8250;</span>
            <a href="/seo/backlinks">Link Building</a>
            <span>&#8250;</span>
            <span>Smartphone &amp; Gadget Posting Sites</span>
          </div>

          <span class="card-icon pillar-icon"><i class="fa-solid fa-mobile-screen-button" aria-hidden="true"></i></span>
          <p class="eyebrow">Link building</p>
          <h1>{title}</h1>
          <p class="pillar-intro">{intro}</p>
          <PageByline />
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="content-body">
        <div class="container article-shell">
          <div class="prose pillar-body">
            <p class="prose-lede">Tech and gadget publishing splits cleanly into three tiers, and where you can land depends entirely on which tier you are aiming at.</p>

            <p>The big news sites do not take guest posts, full stop. The mid-tier phone and reviews sites do. The regional and enthusiast sites are wide open. The list of 100 is below, sorted roughly by that tier.</p>

            <h2 id="tier">Pick your tier first</h2>
            <figure>
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr><th>Tier</th><th>Examples</th><th>How you get in</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr><td>Major tech news</td><td>TechCrunch, The Verge, Wired, Ars Technica, Engadget, CNET</td><td>Not guest posts. Exclusive data, an embargoed launch, or expert commentary on a live story</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Mid-tier phone &amp; reviews</td><td>Android Authority, Android Police, PhoneArena, SlashGear, Pocketnow, GadgetMatch</td><td>Freelance pitches and contributor programmes; hands-on reviews, comparisons, how-tos</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Regional &amp; enthusiast</td><td>91mobiles, Gizmochina, MySmartPrice, MobileSyrup, iGyaan, Tech Gadgets Canada</td><td>Open to contributions; local market angles, launches, buying guides</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Deals, accessories &amp; niche</td><td>The Gadget Flow, Geeky Gadgets, Coolsmartphone, DeviceDaily</td><td>Product-led how-tos, accessory roundups, first-look pieces</td></tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </figure>

            <h2 id="metrics">What the metrics mean</h2>
            <figure>
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr><th>Metric</th><th>What it is</th><th>How much it matters</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr><td>Domain Rating (DR)</td><td>Ahrefs' 0 to 100 score for a whole site's backlink profile</td><td>A useful filter, not a verdict</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Domain Authority (DA)</td><td>Moz's equivalent 0 to 100 score</td><td>Same idea as DR</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Page Authority (PA)</td><td>Moz's score for one specific page</td><td>Matters once you know which page your post will live on</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>URL Rating (UR)</td><td>Ahrefs' score for one specific URL</td><td>Same purpose as PA</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Monthly Traffic</td><td>Estimated organic visits per month</td><td>The most honest signal that a link will be seen</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Spam Score</td><td>Moz's 0 to 4 estimate of how many spam signals a site shows</td><td>A low number is reassurance, not a green light; 3 to 4 means look closer before you pitch</td></tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </figure>

            <div class="callout"><p><strong>Here is the deal:</strong> tech has a lot of high-DR sites, so DR is a weak filter here. What separates a good placement from a wasted one is whether the site's readers buy the kind of device or tool you sell.</p></div>

            <h2 id="list">The 100 tech &amp; gadget sites</h2>
            <p>Domain Rating, Domain Authority, Page Authority, URL Rating, estimated monthly traffic and Spam Score. Every domain was checked live in September 2026; shut-down and redirected sites were swapped for live equivalents. Metrics are third-party estimates and shift over time.</p>
            <figure>
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr><th>#</th><th>Website</th><th>DR</th><th>DA</th><th>PA</th><th>UR</th><th>Monthly traffic</th><th>Spam</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  {SITES.map((s) => (
                    <tr>
                      <td>{s.n}</td>
                      <td>{s.site}</td>
                      <td>{s.dr}</td>
                      <td>{s.da}</td>
                      <td>{s.pa}</td>
                      <td>{s.ur}</td>
                      <td>{s.traffic}</td>
                      <td>{s.spam}</td>
                    </tr>
                  ))}
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </figure>

            <h2 id="pitch">The pitch that works for tech editors</h2>
            <ol>
              <li><strong>Lead with the hands-on or the data.</strong> "I've used this phone as my daily driver for a month" or "we benchmarked 12 chargers" earns the read.</li>
              <li><strong>Pitch the angle the staff missed.</strong> Not "iPhone 17 review". Something like "the iPhone 17 feature nobody is talking about, and why it matters for battery life".</li>
              <li><strong>Bring original assets.</strong> Your own photos, teardown shots, benchmark charts. Tech editors will not run stock imagery.</li>
              <li><strong>Match the tier's format.</strong> News sites want a tight, sourced story; reviews sites want a structured hands-on; regional sites want a local buying angle.</li>
              <li><strong>One clip</strong> in a similar style.</li>
              <li><strong>Disclose any brand or PR relationship</strong> up front.</li>
            </ol>
            <div class="callout"><p><strong>The test:</strong> would an editor run this if you had no product to promote? If yes, pitch it. If it is a walkthrough of your device's features, it goes in the bin.</p></div>

            <h2 id="mistakes">What not to do</h2>
            <ul class="check-list">
              <li>Pitch a generic "top 10 smartphones" listicle. Every site has a rolling one already.</li>
              <li>Send the same pitch to a major news site and a regional gadget blog.</li>
              <li>Submit a review written from the spec sheet without touching the device.</li>
              <li>Stuff affiliate links, or point exact-match anchors ("best budget phone") at your store.</li>
              <li>Use AI-generated filler with no hands-on detail. In tech it is obvious within a paragraph.</li>
            </ul>
            <div class="callout"><p><strong>Bottom line:</strong> a few placements on sites whose readers actually buy your category of device will do more for your rankings and sales than a long list of high-DR general-tech links. Use this list to find your few.</p></div>
          </div>

          <aside class="article-toc">
            <h4>On this page</h4>
            <ul>
              {toc.map((item) => (
                <li><a href={`#${item.id}`}>{item.text}</a></li>
              ))}
            </ul>
          </aside>
        </div>
      </section>

      <div id="faq">
        <Faq items={faqs} />
      </div>

      <section class="section section-alt">
        <div class="container">
          <div class="cta-panel" style="text-align: center; max-width: 720px; margin: 0 auto;">
            <h2>Want us to run the outreach?</h2>
            <p>
              We build the shortlist by tier and category, write the pitches and the pieces, and
              place them on relevant tech sites with real editorial standards. No link farms, no risk.
            </p>
            <div class="hero-actions" style="justify-content: center; margin-top: 0;">
              <a href="/services" class="button">View services</a>
              <a
                href={whatsappUrl("Hi SERP Mentor, I would like help with tech and gadget guest posting and digital PR.")}
                class="button-secondary"
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener noreferrer"
              >
                <i class="fa-brands fa-whatsapp" aria-hidden="true"></i> WhatsApp
              </a>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
    </main>

    <SiteFooter />
  </body>
</html>
